\description{
Calculate one-dimensional accumulated local effects (ALE) of a numeric
covariate, combining all model terms involving that covariate.
}
\details{
Within each interval, predict at its two boundaries for every reference
observation in that interval, retaining its other covariates. Average the
upper-minus-lower prediction differences, then accumulate these averages.
Intervals are right closed, with the minimum included in the first interval.
The returned curve is linearly interpolated between boundaries. Centring
subtracts its mean evaluated at the reference observations, rather than an
unweighted mean over boundaries. Fitting weights do not weight these averages.

On the response scale, transform endpoint predictions before differencing.
Transforming a link-scale ALE curve does not produce response-scale ALE.
Response-scale effects are differences in natural parameter units, not
predicted parameter values, and can be negative.

Distributional families \code{gaulss}, \code{gammals}, \code{gumbls}, \code{gevlss}, \code{shash},
\code{twlss}, and \code{ziplss} are supported. Natural units mean standard deviation
for Gaussian scale, positive scale or dispersion for logged scales, natural
Tweedie power, positive sinh-arcsinh tail parameter, and Poisson rate or
probability of presence for \code{ziplss}. Location parameters need not be response
means. Overall means combining several parameters, multinomial probabilities,
multivariate covariance parameters and grouped-family outputs are unsupported.
For other families, link-scale ALE is available for a single linear predictor;
response-scale ALE requires a supported scalar mean family.

Supported response-scale parameters are:
\itemize{
\item \code{gaulss}: location (mean) and scale (standard deviation);
\item \code{gammals}: location (mean) and scale (dispersion);
\item \code{gumbls} and \code{gevlss}: location and positive scale, plus GEV shape;
\item \code{shash}: location, positive scale, skewness and positive tail parameter
(named \code{kurtosis} to match gratia's parameter names);
\item \code{twlss}: location (mean), power and scale (dispersion);
\item \code{ziplss}: location (Poisson rate) and \code{pi} (probability of presence).
}

Raw-data recovery can require \code{envir} when a model was fitted locally and
its formula no longer retains the environment containing the fitting data.
Alternatively, supply reference \code{data} explicitly.

Coefficient gradients are averaged, accumulated and centred in the same way
as the effects, retaining covariance across intervals and the centring
constant. Link-scale gradients are exact; response-scale intervals use the
delta method. Both interval types condition on the reference observations and
boundaries. Simultaneous coverage concerns the returned boundaries, not all
points between them. No uncertainty for the reference distribution is added.

Formula offsets are included and reevaluated at interval boundaries. Offsets
supplied only through the fitting function's \code{offset} argument are omitted,
following explicit-newdata prediction semantics. BAM predictions use
\code{discrete = FALSE}, including for models fitted with \code{discrete = TRUE}.

Prediction blocks use an existing pool of workers configured with
\code{\link[mirai:daemons]{mirai::daemons()}}, when mirai and carrier are installed. Set \code{block_size}
small enough to give each worker a block; serial and parallel calculations
retain the same block boundaries and accumulation order. Worker startup and
model transfer can outweigh the benefit for small models or reference data.
The caller owns the pool; \code{ale()} does not start or stop workers.
\code{n_cores} controls coefficient simulation for simultaneous intervals, not the
number of prediction workers. Workers use their configured BLAS thread limits.

Data recovery and validation, centring, covariance calculations and interval
simulation remain in the caller. Simultaneous bands use one shared set of
coefficient deviations for all boundaries, preserving results and the
caller's random-number state across worker counts. Models requiring custom
prediction methods, family callbacks, contrasts or formula functions that
workers cannot safely evaluate retain serial prediction. Standard arithmetic
and common base and stats transformations can run on workers; local helpers,
stochastic expressions, custom S3 transformation methods and other package
functions retain serial prediction. Method checks do not invoke active or
lazy caller bindings.

This implementation is written independently from the published ALE
definition, without using code from mgcViz or ALEPlot.
